WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.460
My name is Nick
Tierney, and I'm here

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.350
to talk about the
future of missing data.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:11.520
So I fully hate missing data.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.760
It disrupts your data
analysis because you

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.610
need to stop and work out
how much is missing and think

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:17.960
why is it missing?

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.590
Why?

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.040
Missing data 
controls messages.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.960
I mean, imagine if what I said
just then was bleeped out.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.390
You might get a different
idea of what I was saying.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.360
I redacted hate missing data.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.500
It redacted new data
analysis because redacted

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.320
and work out how redacted
is missing and think

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.030
why redacted and redacted.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.640
Redacted why?

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.000
But the truth is, I
actually love missing data.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.520
In a funny way, understanding.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.770
It's hard, but it has an
extra challenge, a little bit

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.910
of spice to a data analysis.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:50.670
And it's been
something that I found

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:54.600
really exciting and curious
about a data analysis project.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.430
I've actually written
two R packages

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:00.540
to help explore your data
and explore missing data.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.520
And today I'm going to
talk about two things:

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.310
a principle of going from
overview to relationship

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.580
to explore your missing data
and a brief tour of missing data

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.730
visualizations.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:10.930
Emphasis on the brief.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.920
I won't have time to
explore them all in detail.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.040
So the first one I'd
recommend is an overview.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:16.892
viz_miss.

00:01:16.892 --> 00:01:19.350
This gives you a bird's eye
view of your missing data here.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:24.330
The rows, your observations and
the columns are your variables.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.960
If we look at the data here, we
see that the black spots here

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:28.810
are missing.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.840
And the gray are present.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.280
And what we take
away from this is

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.580
that air temperature
C is missing

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.510
and humidity is also missing.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:39.550
They have two big
chunks of missing data.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:42.810
There's also a few times where
there's these black streaks

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:46.500
and that indicates that some
variables go missing together.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.460
We also know that there is
extra information on the amount

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:51.700
of missing image variables.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.390
There's 11% missing in
air temperature and 12%

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.250
missing in humidity.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.708
So now we go from
the overview here

00:01:57.708 --> 00:01:59.208
and we take these
two variables that

00:01:59.208 --> 00:02:01.410
are interesting to us,
that have a lot of missing,

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.800
and we plot them together
to explore a relationship.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.800
We can do this with ggplot
with air temperature

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.240
on the x-axis and
humidity on the y-axis.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.480
But we get a little
interesting problem here,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.750
which says that it's removed 171
rows containing missing values.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:19.720
So how do you show these
values that are removed?

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.820
How do you show missing values

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.570
if they're missing? You can use.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:25.570
geom_miss_point.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:28.230
This is a regular
plot job and this

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.180
will impute the
values of the data

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.300
to be lower than the
range of the data.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.205
So the sort of off to the side
here and in a different color.

00:02:37.205 --> 00:02:38.580
So let's break
this down and look

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.410
at each axis, one at a time.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:44.770
The values in red on the x-axis
are air temperature values

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:47.080
which are missing for humidity.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.570
We see that the
distribution of these values

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:53.620
here matches the first
cluster on the left.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.690
And on the y-axis are
the humidity values

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.670
that have missing air
temperature values.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:59.620
Interesting again here is that
this cluster of values

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.810
seems to match the second
cluster on the right

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.512
of the present values here.

00:03:03.512 --> 00:03:05.470
So what we learn from
this is that missing this

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.570
is a line of these two
classes of air temperature

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:09.040
and humidity.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.010
And let's explore this further
by exploring this relationship

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.220
with another variable.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:16.230
Year. We can just
use facet_wrap year.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.490
Yeah just like a regular plot.

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:21.400
We then get two subplots
here and what we notice

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.400
is something very
interesting that we

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:30.322
get these different values
for all these distributions

00:03:30.322 --> 00:03:32.780
of missingness are shown for
each of these different years.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.738
And we've learned something
really interesting.

00:03:34.738 --> 00:03:38.740
There's a different missing
value pattern for each year.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:41.590
Now I'm going to briefly
explore some more missing data

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.220
visualizations.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.785
So you can use gg_miss_var to
explore the missingness

00:03:46.785 --> 00:03:48.160
in each variable
and you can even

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.920
show this instead of the
number to the percent,

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.620
and you can even explore
this with a facet. Hey, look,

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:54.440
we found it again.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:58.000
The pattern of missing values
for each year in our data set.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.850
You can use gg_miss_upset
to explore combinations

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:05.920
of missingness in simple data
sets or more complex ones.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.860
You can even use gg_miss_fct
to explore

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:12.370
of missing of all variables
grouped by another factor.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:15.010
Here we see of missing
data over all variables

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.100
in this data set for
these different levels

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:21.200
of marital status.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:22.848
Want some help from
the R Consortium

00:04:22.848 --> 00:04:25.140
I'm really happy to say we'll
be adding some more tools

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.970
for visualizing missing data.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.770
I'll briefly talk
about two new additions

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.760
that I think would
be really cool.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:33.390
geom_miss_histogram will show
the amount of missing values

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.800
alongside a univariate
distribution

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.958
by below the range of the data.

00:04:37.958 --> 00:04:39.750
This gives us a general
sense of the amount

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.180
of missing values
in the air temperature

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.150
data here in this example.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.260
geom_imputed_point will
take in a data set

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.010
and identify any imputed values.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:52.560
And will also show the
values that are missing.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.630
And in some very exciting news

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.140
I would love to introduce
a book that I'm working

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.480
with, Allison Horst,
called the missing book.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.260
It contains exercises on missing
data in general workflows

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.790
and on exploring missing
values and imputations.

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:08.820
And the aim is to
have a general book

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.790
to guide you through exploring
your data with some case

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:15.110
studies of real missing data.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:18.170
So I'll wrap up now by saying
that the future of missing data

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:21.110
is its presence, its
presence in software,

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:23.600
in your data analysis
and tutorials,

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.210
and how we think
about data generally.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.610
Funnily enough, missing data
is almost always present.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.680
So let's make sure
we don't forget it.

